      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Definitely a good idea and something I'd like us to do too but you should not be uploading if just testing, it's slower and quite wasteful in comparison to Build & Test.\n\nThere are tools which cover most of the same details of AssetBundleStat which you can get within the unity editor itself such as\n\nUnity's Build Report Inspector:\n\nThis tool gives you a break down of everything within the built asset bundle and can point out what's taking up the most space, clicking on assets listed also takes you right to them in the assets section of the editor allowing you to quickly action on stuff.\n\nhttps://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.build-report-inspector@0.3/manual/index.html\n\n(Can be added in unity's package manager, in unity in the title bar go to Window > Package Manager, click the + in the top left, select Add Package By Name and enter \"com.unity.build-report-inspector\")\n\nThry's Avatar Performance Tools:\n\nGenerally gives a breakdown of various performance metrics of an avatar\n\nhttps://github.com/Thryrallo/VRC-Avatar-Performance-Tools\n\n(Can be added via the VRChat Creator Companion)\n\nAdditionally something not advertised on the main page going over Build & Test is that there's auto-reload functionality. If you are wearing a local test avatar then rebuild it in unity then it will auto reload in-app. This needs to be activated via the launch argument \"--watch-avatars\" (details found at https://docs.vrchat.com/docs/launch-options)",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
